<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Contacts from './Contacts.abide'
import contactsSource from './Contacts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import rowSource from './Row.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two blocks">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Runs</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;script&gt;</code></td><td>once per instance of the component</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;script module&gt;</code></td><td>once for the module, on a server as in a browser</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The difference is what a variable declared in each belongs to. One row's
        <code>calls</code> is that row's; a lookup table every row shares belongs to the module and
        is built once however many rows there are.</p>

        <p>A <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> is module scope, the same as the top of a
        <code>.ts</code> file, so what it exports is the module's: another file imports a helper
        from it as from any module. A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> cannot export, because it runs
        once per instance and an export belongs to no one instance.</p>

        <p>On a server, module scope is shared by every request. A <code>reactive</code> there is
        still one per request, as a <code>reactive</code> is wherever it is declared. A plain
        <code>let</code> is the process's, so state that changes while a page renders belongs in a
        <code>reactive</code>, as <code>opened</code> is below.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Per instance, and once">
        <Example
            id="script-scope"
            title="`<script>` and `<script module>` — per instance, and once"
            summary="Two contact rows from one component. Log a call on the first and read both rows."
            files={{
                'Contacts.abide': contactsSource,
                'Row.abide': rowSource,
            }}
        >
            <Contacts />
        </Example>

        <p><em>2 blocks, 0 conventions</em> — the arm's module scope is whatever happens to sit
        outside the factory, which is where a shared variable ends up by accident rather than by
        decision.</p>

        <p><code>props()</code> is callable from a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block only. A
        <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> block has no instance to read props of, so calling it
        there is a compile error rather than an undefined at runtime.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A setup body does not track">
        <p>Whatever a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> body reads while it runs subscribes to nothing. A
        call written bare in one registers nothing and never follows its arguments, which is why a
        load that has to follow something is wrapped in a <code>reactive</code> body — that wrapper
        is compulsory rather than a matter of taste.</p>

        <p>The same is true of a <strong>branch-local</strong> <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> in a
        block body: it is setup for that item, run once per item, and it tracks nothing either.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/derive-a-value-from-other-values')}>Derived values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A stylesheet a script imports is a dependency of the component">
        <p><code>import './app.css'</code> from any <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> in the file — or
        from any <code>.ts</code> it reaches — makes that stylesheet a dependency of that component,
        so it ships where the component ships and not otherwise.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/scope-styles-to-a-component')}>Styles</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a> — what a
            <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> reads its props from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> —
            code that runs again when a value moves</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/scope-styles-to-a-component')}>Styles</a> — the third block
            a <code>.abide</code> file has</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
